# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses f4c9d13865f3, 2026-10-19
exceeds_pain	two	pain	P3	the pain the wrongful act causes, by the standard set in S11 and S15, without saying who feels it	the pain felt by the wrongdoer, as opposed to the one wronged
more_disgraceful	one
exceeds_evil	one
more_evil	one
# The pain sense shifts in S22. The standard of disgrace in S11 and S15 measures the pain a thing causes, which can be pain to others. S22 to S24 ask instead whether the injurers suffer more than the injured, which is pain to the agent.
# I was unsure about "evil". It may mean harm the act brings about in exceeds_evil, but harm to the agent in Polus's "suffering wrong was more evil" (S18). I kept it as one sense because the text does not mark that difference as clearly as it marks the shift in pain.
